<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siete Partidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1256–1265</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siete Partidas, or Seven-Part Code, is a law code compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under Alfonso X, drawing on Roman and canon law. It covered government, the church, family, property and crime, but gained force of law only through the Ordinance of Alcalá of 1348. It remained a foundation of law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its American empire, and was cited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siete-partidas.jpg" alt="Siete Partidas" width="189" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partidas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
